@adam-bloom asks:

> @evantahler Can you add some details on the table naming plan? Currently, the raw table gets named _airbyte_raw_{stream_name} and the normalized table is named {stream_name}. Your example above merges this - does it just use {stream_name} as the table name then?

Yep! If we go the route of just one table, we'll be using `{stream_name}`, well, technically `{namespace}.{stream_name}`, like we do today.  If we still need to have a raw table, the name could be similar to what it is today.  The goal is for the final table names to remain the same, which we believe to be sensible. We'll be talking more about the table's final format (and name) in another post like this

@nina-j writes:

> In the soft resets section, you mention them being useful for changes to column types. Our use case is rarely type changes, but rather upstream additions of new columns. Can you clarify if this use case is covered as well?

Yes! We aim to support column additions as well with soft resets.  Older records won't have any data for the new columns, but they could be set to NULL.

@pcorbel asks

>  Just one question: where the process will be executed? In the destination (like Snowflake) or locally in the container?  Because for big syncs, I'm afraid of resources problems if we need to store all the data in-memory to deduplicate it. FMHO, the process should leverage the computing power of the destination

Yep! As much of this work as possible will be done using SQL within the destination DB.  This is requirement to work across multiple syncs, as sync 2's containers won't have the information that sync 1's containers saw anyway.

@CamiloAtencio 

> In the case of a full refresh, for whatever reason, can you please write to another table before replacing the old table? This way the user will always have access to the data while the sync runs, which can be for many hours. I think currently you recreate the tables first, leaving the user without any data.

Can you clarify whether you're referring to (current behavior) the `_airbyte_raw_foo` table and/or the normalized `foo` table? I'm under the impression that normalization truncates and inserts new data in a single transaction, but haven't actually doublechecked that.
